WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.120
<v Instructor>Okay, so we've seen Kinesis Data Streams.</v>

2
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.350
Now, Firehose.

3
00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.060
So, Firehose is actually quite easy.

4
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.270
It is to store data into target destinations.

5
00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.340
So we have producers and it could be, again,

6
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.870
a bunch of applications from before

7
00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.410
that can send directly the data into Kinesis Data Firehose,

8
00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:17.837
or Kinesis Data Firehose can read

9
00:00:17.837 --> 00:00:20.100
from your Kinesis Data Streams,

10
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.970
from Amazon CloudWatch or AWS IoT.

11
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.350
But the most common you're going to see

12
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:29.130
is going to be Firehose reading from Kinesis Data Streams.

13
00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.350
So it's going to read the records one by one,

14
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.750
up to one megabyte at a time, okay?

15
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.670
And then if you want to transform the record,

16
00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.470
we could have a Lambda function

17
00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.970
created to transform the record

18
00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.040
to do some small modification.

19
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.810
And then, Kinesis Data Firehose

20
00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.450
will try to fill a big batch of data

21
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.970
to write that data into a target database

22
00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.830
or a target destination, okay?

23
00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.330
So that's why it's called batch writes.

24
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.520
It doesn't write stuff instantaneously.

25
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.640
It will try to batch them to write them efficiently.

26
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.650
So therefore, Kinesis Data Firehose

27
00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.620
is a near real time service.

28
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.530
So in terms of destinations, we have AWS.

29
00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.380
So Amazon S3 is very important.

30
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.200
Amazon Redshift, and to write to Redshift,

31
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.900
Kinesis Data Firehose will first write into Amazon S3,

32
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.360
and then issue a copy command into Redshift

33
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.310
or Amazon OpenSearch.

34
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.260
And you have to remember these three destinations,

35
00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.060
they're very important.

36
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.590
Other destinations include

37
00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.930
third-party destinations from partners

38
00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.144
such as Datadog, Splunk, New Relic, and MongoDB,

39
00:01:28.144 --> 00:01:30.780
but they could be others coming up in the future.

40
00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.025
You don't need to know them.

41
00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:33.720
So this is why we'll not update this slide

42
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:34.560
if there are new partners,

43
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.020
but you can see that there will be new partners over time,

44
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.810
or also there could be a custom destination.

45
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.581
As long as there is a valid HTTP endpoint as an API,

46
00:01:43.581 --> 00:01:48.540
you can have Firehose deliver data into that destination.

47
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.969
And finally, if you have failure for processing

48
00:01:50.969 --> 00:01:53.730
or you want to archive all data going to Firehose,

49
00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.100
it is very much possible to send all

50
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.730
or just a failed data into a backup S3 bucket

51
00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.160
from Kinesis Data Firehose.

52
00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.160
Well, Kinesis Data Firehose is a fully managed service.

53
00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.410
It requires no administration

54
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.240
and it's considered near real time

55
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.320
because you have a buffer

56
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.448
based on the time spent in the buffer

57
00:02:12.448 --> 00:02:14.520
and the size of your buffer.

58
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.250
And then, data is delivered to your destination.

59
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.180
Now, there is the option to disable that buffer now,.

60
00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.010
So what do you use Firehose for?

61
00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.830
Well, you're going to load data into Redshift,

62
00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.383
Amazon S3, OpenSearch, or Splunk.

63
00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.860
You have to remember these four destinations by heart.

64
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.697
So remember Redshift, S3, OpenSearch, and Splunk.

65
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.920
The really cool thing about Kinesis Data Firehose

66
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.260
is that unlike Kinesis data Streams,

67
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.910
there is automatic scaling.

68
00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.380
That means that if you need more throughput,

69
00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.960
Kinesis Data Firehose will scale for you.

70
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.780
And if you need less, it will scale down for you as well.

71
00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.210
It also supports many data formats

72
00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.190
and also is able to do data conversion.

73
00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:54.930
For example, from JSON to Parquet,

74
00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.360
ORC if you use S3.

75
00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.460
The other data transformations you can do

76
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.170
are done through AWS Lambda.

77
00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.480
For example, if you wanted to transform

78
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.580
a CSV into a JSON file,

79
00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.400
we could use AWS Lambda to do that transformation

80
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.280
coupled with Kinesis Data Firehose.

81
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.340
Also supports compression when the target is Amazon S3.

82
00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:15.810
So you're able to compress

83
00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.360
that data being sent into GZIP, ZIP,

84
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.970
or SNAPPY directly into S3.

85
00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.850
And if you load it into Redshift further down the road,

86
00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.050
then GZIP is going to be

87
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.780
the only supported conversion mechanism.

88
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.690
Finally, you're going to pay only for the amount of data

89
00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.670
that goes through Firehose.

90
00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:33.840
And so, the really cool thing

91
00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.870
is that you don't need to provision Firehose in advance

92
00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.820
and you don't get billed for provision capacity.

93
00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.880
You just get billed for used capacity.

94
00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.000
Finally, exam will trick you into thinking

95
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.890
that Spark or the KCL

96
00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.650
can read from KDF or Kinesis Data Firehose.

97
00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:50.820
This is not the case.

98
00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:54.000
Spark streaming and Kinesis Client Library

99
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.620
do not read from Firehose.

100
00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.698
They only read from Kinesis Data Streams.

101
00:03:57.698 --> 00:04:01.500
So I just wanted to make that very, very clear right now.

102
00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.440
Okay, so what does the delivery diagram look like?

103
00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.840
Well, we have Firehose in the middle

104
00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:07.830
and it's going to have a source.

105
00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.450
For example, my source in this example

106
00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.250
is the Kinesis Data Streams.

107
00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.710
So it's going to send that stream into Firehose

108
00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.170
and maybe we'll be able to do some data transformation.

109
00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.390
So remember, I said CSV to JSON.

110
00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.220
Actually, there's a bunch of blueprints

111
00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.170
available for AWS Lambda

112
00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.990
to try help you transform the data in Firehose

113
00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.610
in the format you want.

114
00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.610
Once the data is formatted and transformed,

115
00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.560
we can send the outputs, for example,

116
00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.430
to an Amazon S3 bucket if S3 is your destination.

117
00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.530
Or if Redshift is your destination,

118
00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.152
actually it goes through S3.

119
00:04:39.152 --> 00:04:41.730
And then, there will be a copy command issued

120
00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.460
to put that data into Redshift.

121
00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:45.920
So what about the source records?

122
00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.840
Can we get the source records into an S3 bucket?

123
00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.250
The answer is yes.

124
00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:53.280
Any source record that goes through Kinesis Data Firehose

125
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.480
can be configured

126
00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.120
to land into another bucket of your choice.

127
00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:58.710
So if the exam asks you, okay,

128
00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:00.600
how do we get all the source data

129
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.748
into a Amazon S3 bucket through Kinesis Data Firehose?

130
00:05:03.748 --> 00:05:06.360
Well, this is directly a feature from Firehose.

131
00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.710
There's also a really good feature.

132
00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.840
In case there is a transformation failure,

133
00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.940
we can archive the transformation failure

134
00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:13.710
into an Amazon S3 buckets.

135
00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.560
Or even if there is a delivery failure,

136
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.630
well, we can also have that delivery failure

137
00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.640
put into an Amazon S3 bucket.

138
00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.680
What you remember out of it, out of this diagram,

139
00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.250
is that you don't lose data with Kinesis Data Firehose.

140
00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.131
Either it ends in your targets

141
00:05:28.131 --> 00:05:30.609
or you will have a recollection

142
00:05:30.609 --> 00:05:33.510
of all the transformation failures, the delivery failures,

143
00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:36.150
and even the source records if you wanted to

144
00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.280
into another S3 bucket.

145
00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.830
So remember this diagram, it's really important.

146
00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:42.360
That's basically your understanding

147
00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.460
of Kinesis Data Firehose.

148
00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:48.480
Now we're going to look into how often Kinesis Data Firehose

149
00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.240
sends the output data to the targets.

150
00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.890
And for this, we need to understand

151
00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.050
the Firehose buffer sizing.

152
00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:56.220
So how does Firehose work?

153
00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.300
Well, Firehose will receive

154
00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.670
a bunch of records from the source

155
00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.150
and it will accumulate all these records into a buffer.

156
00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.430
And that buffer, hence this name,

157
00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:06.630
is not flushed all the time.

158
00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:08.610
It's flushed based on some rules.

159
00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.030
It's based on time and size rules.

160
00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:15.210
So we need to define a buffer size,

161
00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.310
for example, 32 megabytes.

162
00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:20.250
That means that if you have enough data from the source

163
00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:24.060
that reach and fill a buffer size of 32 megabytes,

164
00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.910
then, automatically, it's gonna be flushed.

165
00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.680
The other setting is buffer time,

166
00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.480
for example, two minutes.

167
00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.670
That means that if after two minutes,

168
00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.560
your buffer size is not full,

169
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.930
nonetheless it's going to be flushed.

170
00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:40.695
So Firehose, sorry, can automatically increase

171
00:06:40.695 --> 00:06:42.810
the buffer size to increase throughputs.

172
00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:45.300
So it scales really nicely for you

173
00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.730
and accommodates any throughput size.

174
00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.380
So if we have high throughput,

175
00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:53.100
we have a lot of data going through Kinesis Firehose,

176
00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.731
then the buffer size limit will be hit

177
00:06:54.731 --> 00:06:57.180
and will flush based on buffer size.

178
00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.610
But if we have low throughputs, usually,

179
00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:02.040
that means that the buffer size limit will not be hit.

180
00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:05.580
But instead, the buffer time limit will be hit.

181
00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:09.390
Now, buffer time, the minimum you can set is one minute.

182
00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:12.300
And buffer size, you can set a few megabytes.

183
00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.570
Okay, so Kinesis Data Streams versus Firehose.

184
00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:17.550
They looks similar but they're actually not.

185
00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:20.700
Streams is you're going to have to write custom code

186
00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.590
for your producer and your consumer.

187
00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:24.000
And it's going to be real time,

188
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.980
we're going to get 200 millisecond latency

189
00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:27.327
for classic consumers

190
00:07:27.327 --> 00:07:31.080
or 70 millisecond latency for enhanced fan-out consumers.

191
00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.000
You have On-demand or Provisioned mode

192
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.440
for capacity planning.

193
00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.380
You get data storage between 1 to 365 days,

194
00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:41.070
replay capability and multiple consumers.

195
00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.570
You can also use with Lambda

196
00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:46.020
to insert data in real time to OpenSearch, for example.

197
00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:47.610
Firehose, on the other hand,

198
00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.980
is going to be fully managed autoscaling.

199
00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:53.310
It will send data out to S3, Splunk, Redshift,

200
00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:54.900
and OpenSearch for you.

201
00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.120
And it has some serverless data transformation

202
00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:58.980
it can do with AWS Lambda

203
00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.510
and it's near real time.

204
00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:03.420
It's automated scaling and there is no data storage.

205
00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:06.510
So Firehose does not replay data or stuff like this.

206
00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:08.640
So you need to really see the differences between the two.

207
00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.010
To me, they're clear around what they are,

208
00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:12.060
but you have to remember.

209
00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.810
Finally, remember that with the producer or the KPL,

210
00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:17.700
we can produce either into Streams or Firehose.

211
00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:18.630
That is fine.

212
00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:19.740
So remember about your use case.

213
00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:22.560
If your use case is to have applications read data,

214
00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:23.850
maybe it's gonna be Streams.

215
00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:28.410
But if all your use case is about is to send data into S3

216
00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:29.880
or Redshift or Splunk,

217
00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.220
then maybe Firehose is going to be a use case for you.

218
00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.420
And remember, the real time

219
00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:35.790
versus near real time constraints.

220
00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:37.230
So that's it for Firehose.

221
00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:38.063
I hope you liked it

222
00:08:38.063 --> 00:08:39.840
and I will see you in the next lecture.

